from research_schema import F

F('gandaberunda','Gandaberunda: the two-headed bird','The Deccan exhibition catalogue identifies the two-headed bird in the tent-panel context. Museum comparisons document different receptions of this form, including Indian and European heraldic uses. Count heads, bodies and surrounding attributes before identifying a bird; similarity across cloth and stone does not establish a single unchanging meaning.','A two-headed bird on a Dutch grave and one on a Deccani textile are comparable motifs, not automatically the same emblem with the same religious significance.','met-deccan-catalogue npm-gandaberunda met-tent-checklist',aliases='Gandabherunda|two-headed bird')
F('heraldry','Heraldic motifs and their local meanings','The documented palampore comparison shows that a two-headed bird can enter different emblematic systems. In the Dutch cemetery example the grave context matters as much as the motif’s outline. This corpus does not identify a particular family or coat of arms without a legible inscription or catalogue.','A motif with a heraldic use elsewhere is not sufficient evidence to name the owner of this gravestone.','npm-gandaberunda')
F('cloud-collar','Cloud-collar costume and ornamental transfer','The cloud collar is a lobed dress form documented in Chinese textiles. Cammann’s study traces a wider ornamental history and changing interpretations. In the Hampi comparison, the collar-like outline is a visual analogy; it cannot independently establish the performers’ nationality or the route by which a costume travelled.','A queried Chinese identification in a lecture caption remains queried; a similar collar is not an ethnic passport.','tm-cloud cammann-cloud',aliases='cloud collar|yunjian')
F('yali','Yali and composite animal forms','Museum accounts describe yali forms through combinations of lion, elephant or horse-like features and their use in southern architecture. Individual specimens vary. The corpus names visible components before applying the composite label, especially when a trunk, open jaws and smaller figures interlock.','A yali is not a natural zoological species, and every composite creature is not necessarily the same mythological being.','met-yali')
F('dragon','Dragon motifs across Asian arts','Museum teaching material documents movement and transformation of dragon and phoenix motifs between Chinese and Iranian contexts. This supplies a comparative history, not a universal interpretation of every curling creature. Identification still depends on the actual head, limbs and body shown.','A serpentine outline does not establish Chinese origin, a particular deity or a direct import.','met-phoenix')
F('simurgh','Simurgh and narrative identity','In the Shahnameh tradition, the Simurgh protects and raises Zal. A named narrative and its participants support that identification. The course’s fabulous birds should therefore be read with captions and context rather than identified only by size or elaborate plumage.','Simurgh, phoenix and Gandaberunda are not interchangeable names for a fantastic bird.','si-simurgh met-phoenix')
F('animal-motifs','Animal motifs: identification before symbolism','Animals can function as narrative participants, compositional accents, attributes or decoration. Their presence in Islamic-world arts is well documented. This corpus keeps the visible species or uncertain form separate from an interpretation; it assigns no automatic political, moral or religious meaning to every animal.','An animal’s recurrence across different objects does not prove identical symbolism, shared patronage or a common workshop.','met-figural')
F('iconographic-attributes','Attributes, gestures and identification','A deity or spiritual figure can be identified through the combination of held objects, pose, adornment and context. Multiple arms are one representational device, not a diagnosis of one particular deity. Exact attribute combinations matter more than the general category of elaborate figure.','A crown, weapon or many arms alone cannot securely distinguish every deity or identify a human sitter.','met-deities met-hindu')
F('lotus','Lotus and sacred imagery','The lotus can serve as an attribute, blossom or stylized pedestal, with associations of spiritual perfection in South Asian religious art. Its location and pictorial function matter. A general floral form or building nickname is not enough to establish the same symbolic reading.','A lotus pedestal, a flower held in a hand and the name Lotus Mahal are different uses of a term.','met-deities')
F('durga-iconography','Durga and the buffalo-demon narrative','Durga’s many weapons and the buffalo or emerging human demon identify a specific narrative of divine victory. A documented museum sculpture shows weapons associated with several gods. Regional and medium-specific versions differ, so do not transfer every weapon count from that sculpture to the lecture print.','Durga defeating Mahisha is not interchangeable with Kali standing over Shiva; identify the opponent and attributes.','met-durga')
F('kali-tara','Kali and Tara in the Calcutta diptych','The Met identifies the paired print as Kali and Tara, wrathful protectors in a Mahavidya context. Their appearance, supporting figures and surroundings differ within the same sheet. Preserve the left/right distinction and the specific visual attributes rather than treating both halves as duplicated images of one goddess.','Shared fierce attributes do not erase the distinction between Kali and Tara; chromolithography names their printed medium.','met-kali-tara')
F('shiva-iconography','Shiva-related imagery in the lecture','The lecture captions identify Shiva-related subjects in popular prints and paintings. The Kali/Tara catalogue also identifies Shiva in subordinate positions within that particular diptych. Other linked images must be read through their own captions and attributes rather than borrowing the diptych’s narrative.','A recumbent male figure or a trident does not, by itself, authenticate every identification as Shiva.','met-kali-tara met-hindu')
F('ramayana','Ramayana imagery across cloth and painting','The lecture explicitly identifies Ramayana subject matter and shows Rama, Sita and attendant figures within framed textile narratives. The central enthroned group and surrounding registers are observable organizing devices. This research pass did not establish every small episode or figure in the textile; those remain unnamed.','A Ramayana subject does not dictate one medium, workshop or arrangement; do not give every small panel a guessed episode title.',search='research25',status='draft')
F('jain-iconography','Jina imagery and unresolved print identification','Museum documentation establishes Jinas as central figures in Jain devotional imagery, with details of dress and attendant figures varying. That comparative knowledge does not settle the identity of the provisionally captioned Pune print in this corpus. Preserve its uncertainty at object level.','A seated meditating figure is insufficient to distinguish Mahavira from every other Jina or from a Buddhist figure.','lacma-jain')
F('sarasvati','Sarasvati and Bijapuri musical devotion','The Walters record connects Sarasvati with the musical devotion of Ibrahim Adil Shah II. This supports a religious and courtly context for comparison. It is not the catalogue of the slide’s enthroned painting and therefore cannot supply that painting’s date, ownership or exact commission.','A Hindu goddess in a Deccani courtly setting does not prove the patron’s conversion or erase the specificity of the devotional subject.','walters-sarasvati')
F('yogini-andal','Yogini, Andal and a questioned identification','The lecture asks how the bird, hair arrangement, jewellery and ascetic-looking dress should be read in Yogini with a Mynah. Its alternatives are part of the interpretive problem. The source search did not resolve the lecture’s Andal identification or proposed porcelain connection securely enough to promote either to fact.','A bird and jewellery do not alone prove that the figure is Andal, a queen or a portrait of a named woman.',search='research10',status='draft')
F('nafs-majnun','Ascetic and nag: self-discipline and alternative readings','The Deccan catalogue discusses interpretive alternatives around the thin rider and horse, including spiritual discipline and literary associations. These are interpretations of the image, not translations of a legible caption in the slide. The skeletal animal and marbled treatment remain firmer observations than one exclusive allegory.','Nag here names a horse, not a naga serpent; nafs-related interpretation and a Majnun identification are not interchangeable certainties.','met-deccan-catalogue')
F('silsila','Silsila: spiritual transmission','A silsila is a lineage of spiritual transmission through Sufi masters. The course uses chains of relation to interpret portraits and gatherings. Distinguish that historical or spiritual connection from a claim that all figures were physically present together when the image was made.','A painted lineage is not automatically documentary group portraiture or proof of a single meeting.','met-sufi')
F('hero-stone','Hero stones and memorial registers','Museum examples document memorial stones organized into registers. A lower action scene and an upper transformed or commemorative setting must be read together. This comparative pattern helps frame the Hampi example but cannot identify an otherwise unnamed person or establish the circumstances of death.','Hero stones and sati memorials are distinct categories; similar stone format does not make their subjects identical.','goa-hero')
F('chakravartin','Chakravartin: universal sovereignty','The wheel-turning universal monarch is a political and religious concept that entered Buddhist imagery. In this corpus it provides a comparative vocabulary for sovereignty, not proof that every ruler represented on a globe bears this exact historical title.','A conceptual parallel in universal rule does not establish direct derivation of one imperial image from another tradition.','met-chakravartin',aliases='cakravartin|wheel-turning monarch')
F('cosmic-globe','The globe as an imperial and cosmic stage','Jahangir’s allegories place the ruler on a mapped sphere and subordinate other beings or rulers through scale and position. The Smithsonian record identifies the bull and fish support in the Malik Ambar image; scholarship treats the Shah Abbas embrace as hierarchical imperial imagery.','These are visual claims to authority, not photographs of real meetings, military victories or literal positions in space.','si-malik whc-walrus')
F('owl','The owl in Jahangir’s Malik Ambar allegory','A scholarly study connects the owl beside the target with hostile and occult imagery directed against Malik Ambar. The image imagines a triumph that Jahangir had not achieved. The bird’s exact placement belongs to the visual analysis; this interpretation is specific to the documented political context.','The owl here should not be assigned a universal wisdom symbolism imported from an unrelated tradition.','owl-study')
F('lion-lamb','Lion and lamb in imperial allegory','In the Jahangir–Shah Abbas comparison, the animals and unequal rulers contribute to an idealized, hierarchical order. Scholarship situates the globe and privileged imperial position within claims of sovereignty. The peaceful pairing does not make the rulers equal in the composition.','An embrace and adjacent animals are not documentary evidence of an equal alliance or an actual meeting.','whc-walrus lacma-poverty')
F('halo-angels','Halos, angels and adapted courtly imagery','The Shah Jahan portrait catalogue documents European angel imagery within an imperial Mughal composition. Such figures can be adapted to glorify a ruler. A halo or winged attendant therefore needs local pictorial context rather than an automatic reading as Christian worship.','Christian-derived visual devices do not establish that the represented ruler or patron adopted Christianity.','met-shahjahan')
F('balance','Balances: weighing, value and allegory','The Goldsmith painting contains a working balance in a shop setting. The lecture also reads a balance within Jahangir’s allegorical apparatus. The same recognizable object can serve practical measurement in one image and a claim about justice in another; the latter is the course’s interpretation.','A shared balance does not give both pictures the same occupation, narrative or political message.','met-goldsmith')
F('mirror','Convex mirror and the pictured world','The Met identifies the convex mirror and reflected men in A Goldsmith in his Shop and discusses a possible moral reading. Its curved reflection opens a view beyond the central transaction. The moral interpretation remains qualified rather than an unquestionable answer key.','The reflected scene is not proof of a named sitter, and a later-added halo does not securely identify the goldsmith as a saint.','met-goldsmith')
F('luna','Luna: planetary engraving and album reuse','The NGV catalogues Beham’s Luna as a 1539 engraving in a planetary series, with a crescent and Cancer sign. The course shows such a European print remounted among Persian drawings. The standalone design’s catalogue is not the provenance of the complete Gulshan composite sheet.','The date or authorship of a print fragment does not date or attribute every component of a later album page.','ngv-luna')
F('pineapple','Pineapple motifs and changing contexts','MAP and the Museum of Christian Art compare a kalamkari palampore with a chasuble through the pineapple motif and emphasize changing meanings. Their published description supports the comparison, not a transcript-level claim about every detail. An exact plant identification in a slide still requires a resolved form.','A shared pineapple motif does not mean a trade textile and a liturgical garment had identical functions or symbolism.','map-pineapple')
F('kabai-kullayi','Kabai and kullayi: courtly dress','Wagoner’s scholarship and the silk-history study discuss the long tunic and conical cap as Islamicate forms adopted within Vijayanagara courtly presentation. The Lepakshi example connects clothing to political self-fashioning and craft exchange. Dress form and personal religious identity remain different questions.','Adopting a garment associated with another courtly culture does not prove the wearer’s conversion or foreign birth.','wagoner-dress mpg-silk',aliases='kabayi|kullayi')
F('costume','Costume as evidence, with limits','Clothing can communicate rank, ritual role and participation in wider courtly fashions. The Vijayanagara dress studies provide a documented example of adopted forms. For an unnamed figure, a costume resemblance remains an observation unless a caption or specific historical source supports a stronger identity claim.','Do not infer nationality, ethnicity or belief directly from a hat, collar or robe.','wagoner-dress mpg-silk')
F('habshi','African participation in Deccani courts','The V&A account of Malik Ambar supplies a documented example of African political power in India. The lecture separately identifies Khawwas-Khan as an Abyssinian commander. These are distinct individuals; the comparative source does not independently catalogue Khawwas-Khan’s portrait.','Habshi-related context does not make every African courtier Malik Ambar, nor does a European collector become the portrait’s original patron.','va-africans')
